 and go oh my god  there's even more of this thing that I  already like that's terrific  and and reading the book afterwards  doesn't diminish the experience of the  movie because I already have my positive  experience with it certainly the first  time and it's easier to recapture on  subsequent real watches if that first  one wasn't diminished by sitting there  going well they didn't use that line oh  they cut that character but I liked them  oh they change that and maybe if you're  someone who can shut off the analytical  part of your brain better than I can and  that doesn't happen to you maybe it  doesn't make more sense to read the book  first for me just doesn't it it  diminishes one of the experiences now  again I did mention a movie style books  table if you are a complete book snob  and care more about the experience of  reading than you do about the experience  of watching movie then yeah that makes  it an easy call for you I enjoy both i  watch a lot more movies than I read  books but I that again has more to do  with me being slightly lazy and time  management I've had books impact me just  as intensely and deeply as any movie  ever  has so a couple of specific examples  just to sort of give you a sense of  where I'm at one of my favorite books  ever is The Hitchhiker's Guide to the  galaxy  now the guide to the galaxy movie is  kind of divisive but I feel that in many  ways it's a pretty good movie what I  hate in it though and when I and I I've  gotten fairly cautious about using the  term hate I try and be restrictive of  when I use that but I hate the character  of Zaphod Beeblebrox in the movie and  it's not because he's a bad character or  he's badly written or even badly  performed despite the fact I don't  really like Sam Rockwell in the film  he's fine good even but the reason I  hate him in that movie is because he is  my favorite character from that book  series and they missed key essential  elements to his character they  simplified him  they made him stupid and in the books  he's not stupid he's lazy he doesn't  want to have to think about stuff if he  can get someone else to figure it out  for him he'll do that but he's not an  idiot and the movie made him an idiot  and I hate it but the only reason I hate  is because I'd already read the book and  I know how much better that version of  the character was watching the second  Harry Potter film and I actually do like  you the Harry Potter films and in fact  the fifth Harry Potter film is one of  the few instances where I liked the  movie notably better than the book even  after having read the book first but in  the second film I was taken out of the  climax by the absence of one little  exchange between Harry and  the sort of projection of Tom Riddle  from the diary there's just oh and I  forget the exact wording now I should  have looked it up but yeah lazy ya  notice look at that I like a lazy  character in a book can't imagine one  but Harry Tots Tom because Tom Rizal you  know you just feed it the greatest was  wizard that ever existed you know  basically talking about his own future  self and Harry mocks him and taunts him  by saying I've seen what you are now  your parasite you you are forced to  leech off another to even continue to  exist basically mocking this idea that  Voldemort became a great wizard and  stent talking about what he was reduced  to and what we saw in Sorcerer's Stone  and I love that I love that moment for  Harry in terms of a moment of strength  and standing his ground I love the way  he cuts to the heart of this particular  projection of Voldemort this young  teenage ambitious Tom Riddle I love that  it's so much and it's not in the movie  and that doesn't break the movie that  doesn't break the scene but it does mean  that I got taken out of it cuz oh there  was something that would be there  wouldn't it made the scene better maybe  a little high little I get more but it  the scene didn't suddenly fail because  that was